00:42:45,058 --> 00:42:49,340
[SPEAKER_01]: I threw two fastballs, one across field, overhand.

605
00:42:51,140 --> 00:42:52,841
[SPEAKER_01]: That's just straight fastball.

606
00:42:53,902 --> 00:43:05,707
[SPEAKER_01]: I learned early in my career, I learned to turn the ball over, catch the ball in the narrow part of the ball with the fingers out a little more, grip it tight.

607
00:43:06,387 --> 00:43:08,128
[SPEAKER_01]: And I threw a little dinky center.

608
00:43:09,449 --> 00:43:10,509
[SPEAKER_01]: That was my best skill.

609
00:43:10,923 --> 00:43:12,523
[SPEAKER_01]: practice.

610
00:43:13,784 --> 00:43:26,568
[SPEAKER_01]: But if, for example, a man on third and when I fly ball or something, I get two strikes, I would come across the seam and try to ride and pass the ball, put the strike down.

611
00:43:27,288 --> 00:43:32,650
[SPEAKER_01]: But the majority of the pitches I play are with the seams, with a little bit of the seam.

612
00:43:33,130 --> 00:43:36,151
[SPEAKER_01]: The ball is round, the bat's round, they tell you to hit it square.

613
00:43:36,171 --> 00:43:37,831
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't have to have the big foot.

614
00:43:39,132 --> 00:43:40,292
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the quick break

615
00:43:41,538 --> 00:43:49,722
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, I didn't have any kind of a sinking ball like Bob Stanley or some of these real sinker ball pitchers.

616
00:43:49,742 --> 00:43:53,084
[SPEAKER_01]: My ball was quicker and harder.

617
00:43:53,264 --> 00:43:54,605
[SPEAKER_01]: How fast did you throw?

618
00:43:54,645 --> 00:43:56,766
[SPEAKER_02]: Did they measure speeds in those days?

619
00:43:56,866 --> 00:43:57,426
[SPEAKER_01]: They didn't.

620
00:43:57,486 --> 00:43:58,427
[SPEAKER_01]: They tried.

621
00:43:58,647 --> 00:43:59,707
[SPEAKER_01]: It wasn't accurate.

622
00:43:59,827 --> 00:44:04,470
[SPEAKER_01]: They had these ray guns now that can tell you exactly.

623
00:44:04,810 --> 00:44:06,491
[SPEAKER_01]: They tried to clock Bob Feller.

624
00:44:06,511 --> 00:44:09,512
[SPEAKER_01]: They said they clocked him at 100 miles an hour or thereabouts.

625
00:44:11,450 --> 00:44:12,551
[SPEAKER_01]: It wasn't accurate.

626
00:44:12,571 --> 00:44:13,992
[SPEAKER_01]: They said it wasn't accurate.

627
00:44:14,012 --> 00:44:20,037
[SPEAKER_01]: There's some way they had to throw the ball, they had to go through here, break this and break this, and the ball went through.

628
00:44:20,257 --> 00:44:21,318
[SPEAKER_01]: They timed it that way.

629
00:44:21,739 --> 00:44:22,419
[SPEAKER_01]: It wasn't accurate.

630
00:44:23,020 --> 00:44:30,226
[SPEAKER_01]: But they said that the best, the closest they could get, Cullars could throw about a hundred miles an hour.

631
00:44:33,729 --> 00:44:38,833
[SPEAKER_01]: And there's some, Nolan Ryan can throw about, or did throw about as far as Cullars.

632
00:44:40,312 --> 00:44:47,496
[SPEAKER_01]: And there's a lot of guys now that are throwing 95 to 97 pretty active.

633
00:44:47,516 --> 00:44:55,240
[SPEAKER_01]: That's Roger Clements, Red Sox, and this and that kind of thing, that are throwing 95 to 97.

634
00:44:55,280 --> 00:45:00,844
[SPEAKER_01]: Bruce Gossage, you know, one of these, those kind of guys throwing 96, 97.

635
00:45:03,905 --> 00:45:06,947
[SPEAKER_01]: I could throw except for fellas.

636
00:45:09,302 --> 00:45:16,086
[SPEAKER_01]: Within the top five pitchers, I could throw right along with them.

637
00:45:16,126 --> 00:45:17,807
[SPEAKER_02]: You say it'd be over 90 probably?

638
00:45:18,007 --> 00:45:18,928
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, definitely.

639
00:45:20,388 --> 00:45:26,312
[SPEAKER_01]: In other words, if there's anybody else throwing over 90, I could throw just right along.

640
00:45:27,773 --> 00:45:33,096
[SPEAKER_01]: The main thing that I had going for me was control.

641
00:45:33,976 --> 00:45:35,237
[SPEAKER_01]: I had excellent control.

642
00:45:37,334 --> 00:45:38,454
[SPEAKER_01]: I didn't walk anybody.

643
00:45:38,634 --> 00:45:39,675
[SPEAKER_01]: Never wild-fished.

644
00:45:39,995 --> 00:45:41,815
[SPEAKER_01]: Never wild-fished a man.

645
00:45:42,095 --> 00:45:44,056
[SPEAKER_01]: Never even wild-fished a man to settle.

646
00:45:44,556 --> 00:45:45,976
[SPEAKER_01]: Never.

647
00:45:47,317 --> 00:45:48,877
[SPEAKER_01]: Very seldom walked anybody.

648
00:45:49,637 --> 00:45:52,058
[SPEAKER_00]: How did you acquire it?

649
00:45:52,078 --> 00:45:55,099
[SPEAKER_01]: Natural ability, I guess.

650
00:45:56,719 --> 00:46:00,780
[SPEAKER_01]: And from the time I was six, seven years old, I liked to throw.

651
00:46:00,820 --> 00:46:02,921
[SPEAKER_01]: I was throwing rocks and throwing at everything.

652
00:46:03,621 --> 00:46:06,142
[SPEAKER_01]: Walked three miles to school on a gravel road.

653
00:46:07,237 --> 00:46:10,479
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm not supposed to throw anything with gravel rocks.

654
00:46:11,080 --> 00:46:13,421
[SPEAKER_01]: Throwing at rabbits and birds and everything else.

655
00:46:14,382 --> 00:46:16,683
[SPEAKER_01]: But a lot of it is natural ability.

656
00:46:17,924 --> 00:46:20,766
[SPEAKER_01]: My dad could throw well.

657
00:46:20,806 --> 00:46:22,987
[SPEAKER_01]: He played some semi-pro ball way back.

658
00:46:24,328 --> 00:46:27,650
[SPEAKER_01]: And my mother was kind of outdoored.

659
00:46:28,611 --> 00:46:31,293
[SPEAKER_01]: She was accurate with his coordination.

660
00:46:32,934 --> 00:46:33,754
[SPEAKER_01]: Some of it, yeah.

661
00:46:34,234 --> 00:46:35,335
[SPEAKER_01]: You can train yourself.

662
00:46:35,775 --> 00:46:36,476
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't know why.

663
00:46:37,032 --> 00:46:40,993
[SPEAKER_01]: But I've seen a lot of pictures, with all their belts in the world.

664
00:46:42,794 --> 00:46:44,715
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you can't control it, it's no good.

665
00:46:45,155 --> 00:46:50,016
[SPEAKER_01]: Like Bill Zubrin here, Detroit had a boy that could throw as hard as anybody.

666
00:46:50,436 --> 00:46:55,858
[SPEAKER_01]: Or this fella, I don't care who, Luke Kretlow, and he'd never beat anybody hard enough.

667
00:46:56,738 --> 00:47:01,020
[SPEAKER_01]: Ken Chase, a left-handed pitcher that we had, everybody tried.

668
00:47:01,580 --> 00:47:04,381
[SPEAKER_01]: Ken Chase, a left-handed pitcher, could throw as hard as anybody.

669
00:47:04,747 --> 00:47:06,408
[SPEAKER_01]: But he couldn't get it over, consistently.

670
00:47:06,468 --> 00:47:22,076
[SPEAKER_00]: I remember another one of the Indians, a white name, I can't remember his name.

671
00:47:22,236 --> 00:47:22,736
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, yeah.

672
00:47:22,756 --> 00:47:23,257
[SPEAKER_01]: Back off.

673
00:47:23,277 --> 00:47:25,338
[SPEAKER_01]: It takes so much time to break this.

674
00:47:25,958 --> 00:47:33,982
[SPEAKER_01]: Either, what I was thinking, either I had somebody beat and over with it in two hours,

675
00:47:34,562 --> 00:47:37,463
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, I was gone earlier, wasn't taking a shot.

676
00:47:38,664 --> 00:47:42,746
[SPEAKER_01]: But I like, not real fast, but I want it to pitch.

677
00:47:44,066 --> 00:47:48,468
[SPEAKER_01]: If I'm going to, I want the catcher to get me that ball back.

678
00:47:48,728 --> 00:47:50,269
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't want any distractions.

679
00:47:50,789 --> 00:47:58,313
[SPEAKER_01]: If I just miss with a pitch, the quicker I can get it back and concentrating on where that ball is, the better.

680
00:47:58,353 --> 00:48:01,414
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't want to do all this a lot, you know.

681
00:48:03,048 --> 00:48:04,929
[SPEAKER_01]: Enfielders coming in and all that.

682
00:48:05,210 --> 00:48:06,811
[SPEAKER_02]: How did the umpires treat you?

683
00:48:07,251 --> 00:48:10,213
[SPEAKER_02]: Or how did you... Have any problems with umpires?

684
00:48:10,233 --> 00:48:10,533
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

685
00:48:11,994 --> 00:48:12,895
[SPEAKER_01]: Not particularly.

686
00:48:16,797 --> 00:48:17,438
[SPEAKER_01]: I used to...

687
00:48:18,819 --> 00:48:21,780
[SPEAKER_01]: I tried to get along with the umpire and the sports officer.

688
00:48:23,021 --> 00:48:25,983
[SPEAKER_01]: I was always kidding with the umpire a little bit.

689
00:48:26,944 --> 00:48:28,305
[SPEAKER_01]: Trying to be a good guy.

690
00:48:30,306 --> 00:48:31,527
[SPEAKER_01]: Wasn't begging for anything.

691
00:48:31,987 --> 00:48:33,208
[SPEAKER_01]: But they're human, I mean.

692
00:48:34,468 --> 00:48:36,470
[SPEAKER_01]: Umpires have got to think this job.

693
00:48:37,630 --> 00:48:38,851
[SPEAKER_01]: A real tough job.

694
00:48:39,791 --> 00:48:48,016
[SPEAKER_01]: If I pitch a nine-inning ballgame, I hardly ever threw a hundred pitches.

695
00:48:48,096 --> 00:48:51,858
[SPEAKER_01]: Anywhere from 85 to 95 for real.

696
00:48:53,099 --> 00:49:00,743
[SPEAKER_01]: But out of that 95 pitches, I would make, if I were my own judge, I threw an umpire in my own ballgame,

697
00:49:02,378 --> 00:49:05,959
[SPEAKER_01]: I've made anywhere from five to ten pitches for that ball.

698
00:49:06,419 --> 00:49:09,120
[SPEAKER_01]: But I don't know whether it was a strike or a ball.

699
00:49:09,800 --> 00:49:11,840
[SPEAKER_01]: The outside part of the plate is here.

700
00:49:12,680 --> 00:49:17,202
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't know whether that ball was high enough or whether it hit a part of the plate.

701
00:49:18,602 --> 00:49:19,782
[SPEAKER_01]: But he's got the coach.

702
00:49:20,222 --> 00:49:21,643
[SPEAKER_00]: The man back here's got the coach.

703
00:49:21,883 --> 00:49:22,883
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't know myself.

704
00:49:23,843 --> 00:49:31,165
[SPEAKER_01]: If I were on par in my own game, in my own honesty, I don't know what this ball is, sir.

705
00:49:31,245 --> 00:49:31,945
[SPEAKER_01]: I've seen this.

706
00:49:32,953 --> 00:49:34,934
[SPEAKER_01]: The Red Sox sitting here.

707
00:49:34,954 --> 00:49:37,135
[SPEAKER_01]: Twenty-five players on a team, nine players.

708
00:49:37,935 --> 00:49:39,216
[SPEAKER_01]: Bunch of us sitting on a bench.

709
00:49:39,236 --> 00:49:40,477
[SPEAKER_01]: First base is right here.

710
00:49:41,217 --> 00:49:43,198
[SPEAKER_01]: I've seen a close play in first base.

711
00:49:44,439 --> 00:49:46,220
[SPEAKER_01]: The six of us sitting here looking at it.

712
00:49:46,760 --> 00:49:49,281
[SPEAKER_01]: Three of us said he was out and three of us said he was safe.

713
00:49:50,262 --> 00:49:53,083
[SPEAKER_01]: In our own hearts and minds, you know, just discussing.

714
00:49:53,803 --> 00:49:56,345
[SPEAKER_01]: That poor devil got to college, right then and there.

715
00:49:58,506 --> 00:50:00,106
[SPEAKER_01]: On a real close play, you know.

716
00:50:00,146 --> 00:50:01,167
[SPEAKER_01]: No instant replay.

717
00:50:03,362 --> 00:50:06,503
[SPEAKER_01]: Vampires make mistakes, no doubt about it.

718
00:50:06,783 --> 00:50:12,124
[SPEAKER_02]: You had such good control, though, that there probably wasn't that much of a problem with vampires.

719
00:50:12,825 --> 00:50:15,806
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, I'll tell you what.

720
00:50:16,606 --> 00:50:17,946
[SPEAKER_01]: I kind of had a groove.

721
00:50:19,687 --> 00:50:22,908
[SPEAKER_01]: I pitched left-handers right on the outside for it.

722
00:50:25,108 --> 00:50:31,170
[SPEAKER_01]: And the only objection when I asked, the only play where I cried about it,

723
00:50:32,481 --> 00:50:36,342
[SPEAKER_01]: left-hand hitters up here, and I'm fixing outside the umpire looking over.

724
00:50:37,722 --> 00:50:39,502
[SPEAKER_01]: They have given me some pictures out there.

725
00:50:39,662 --> 00:50:42,763
[SPEAKER_01]: It's never hard to take one away.

726
00:50:43,703 --> 00:50:47,984
[SPEAKER_00]: But I get two strikes, and I come in on a left-hander.

727
00:50:49,104 --> 00:50:53,585
[SPEAKER_01]: And I get what I think is a good portion of the play above the net.

728
00:50:53,745 --> 00:50:55,345
[SPEAKER_01]: And first, I didn't get it.

729
00:50:55,725 --> 00:50:58,666
[SPEAKER_01]: That was a pitch I needed for a strikeout with two strikes.

730
00:50:59,335 --> 00:51:01,276
[SPEAKER_01]: I talked to him for a second.

731
00:51:02,918 --> 00:51:07,681
[SPEAKER_01]: I said, you've given me these pictures, you don't question me about that.

732
00:51:09,062 --> 00:51:10,283
[SPEAKER_01]: But I said, I don't know.

733
00:51:13,545 --> 00:51:15,786
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the plot that he had going on.

734
00:51:18,848 --> 00:51:27,654
[SPEAKER_02]: Did Ted Williams get the break on some doubtful pictures you think because he had such a good eye?

735
00:51:27,975 --> 00:51:28,535
[SPEAKER_01]: Probably.

736
00:51:29,985 --> 00:51:31,586
[SPEAKER_01]: with certain umpires.

737
00:51:32,286 --> 00:51:33,566
[SPEAKER_01]: In other words, a lot of people.

738
00:51:35,127 --> 00:51:38,909
[SPEAKER_01]: The opposition said Ted umpired his own ballgame.

739
00:51:40,149 --> 00:51:46,972
[SPEAKER_01]: But he did know the strike zone, and he would not hit a black ball under any circumstances for anybody.

740
00:51:49,413 --> 00:51:50,573
[SPEAKER_01]: He knew the strike zone.

741
00:51:50,593 --> 00:51:51,694
[SPEAKER_01]: He was in that groove.

742
00:51:54,495 --> 00:51:59,777
[SPEAKER_01]: And if it's close, they were with Ted,

743
00:52:02,213 --> 00:52:08,098
[SPEAKER_01]: The umpire, not Latin, not old, but I'm sure he got some.

744
00:52:08,778 --> 00:52:14,143
[SPEAKER_01]: For example, I was playing, I was pitching one day.

745
00:52:15,083 --> 00:52:18,126
[SPEAKER_01]: Jack Kramer was pitching against me from St. Louis Browns.

746
00:52:18,686 --> 00:52:20,208
[SPEAKER_01]: Later came over to Boston.

747
00:52:21,929 --> 00:52:29,335
[SPEAKER_01]: And the first time up, the first pitch, he threw me a perfect curveball.

748
00:52:33,516 --> 00:52:35,557
[SPEAKER_01]: What I thought was a perfect pitch.

749
00:52:36,618 --> 00:52:41,781
[SPEAKER_01]: I think they must have taken a picture of it because he threw that same pitch right at the same place every time.

750
00:52:42,582 --> 00:52:49,366
[SPEAKER_01]: But the umpire called me out on a pitch that was outside.

751
00:52:50,987 --> 00:52:54,669
[SPEAKER_01]: And I told the umpire, I said, that wasn't a strike, the ball was outside.

752
00:52:55,630 --> 00:52:56,370
[SPEAKER_01]: You know what he said?

753
00:52:56,790 --> 00:52:58,151
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, it's just a matter of time.

754
00:52:58,171 --> 00:52:59,172
[SPEAKER_01]: You ain't going to hit it.

755
00:53:01,093 --> 00:53:05,335
[SPEAKER_02]: get it over with, you know.

756
00:53:05,355 --> 00:53:08,276
[SPEAKER_01]: So that's the difference, you know, about the umpires.

757
00:53:08,676 --> 00:53:11,217
[SPEAKER_01]: In other words, with some umpires, it don't make any difference.

758
00:53:11,297 --> 00:53:13,998
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm not a good hitter, but just get it over with.

759
00:53:15,479 --> 00:53:18,620
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, I'll tell you a story about that too.

760
00:53:18,640 --> 00:53:20,161
[SPEAKER_00]: When Yogi first came out,

761
00:53:29,898 --> 00:53:32,480
[SPEAKER_01]: He had the reputation of being a good fastball hitter.

762
00:53:33,381 --> 00:53:36,703
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, don't let him beat you with a fastball.

763
00:53:37,564 --> 00:53:37,904
[SPEAKER_01]: All right.

764
00:53:39,105 --> 00:53:42,828
[SPEAKER_01]: We get into a ballgame against the Yankees, been waiting for it.

765
00:53:43,329 --> 00:53:45,730
[SPEAKER_01]: The wind's blowing in, he's left-handed.

766
00:53:47,452 --> 00:53:54,537
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's about to get six in, and I got a four-nothing lead, two out and nobody on, and Yogi Bear comes in.

767
00:53:55,979 --> 00:53:57,740
[SPEAKER_01]: This is the first game that I've seen.

768
00:53:59,275 --> 00:54:04,217
[SPEAKER_01]: The catcher called for the curveball, and I shook him off, shook him off, got the fastball.

769
00:54:05,197 --> 00:54:06,877
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm going to find out now.

770
00:54:07,598 --> 00:54:08,818
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm a fastball pitcher.

771
00:54:09,258 --> 00:54:10,398
[SPEAKER_01]: He's a fastball hitter.

772
00:54:10,879 --> 00:54:15,960
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm going to find out whether my fastball is good enough to get him out.

773
00:54:17,341 --> 00:54:21,402
[SPEAKER_01]: The first pitch, he fouled it fast.

774
00:54:22,822 --> 00:54:26,423
[SPEAKER_01]: The catcher went through the same thing again, gave me everything but the fastball.

775
00:54:26,463 --> 00:54:28,284
[SPEAKER_01]: I shook him off till I got the fastball.

776
00:54:29,035 --> 00:54:33,858
[SPEAKER_01]: I came back with the fastball, above the knees, down the middle of the plate, that's me.

777
00:54:33,938 --> 00:54:34,979
[SPEAKER_01]: I was throwing hard.

778
00:54:36,400 --> 00:54:41,864
[SPEAKER_01]: He hit the thing and the teeth of the wind into the base of the bullpen and turned away hard.

779
00:54:43,625 --> 00:54:47,147
[SPEAKER_01]: To this day, I haven't thrown hard to him since.

780
00:54:47,548 --> 00:54:51,050
[SPEAKER_01]: A lot of people say he's a bad ball hitter.

781
00:54:54,968 --> 00:54:56,269
[SPEAKER_01]: He was a fastball hitter.

782
00:54:56,429 --> 00:54:57,730
[SPEAKER_01]: He wasn't a bad ball hitter.

783
00:54:57,810 --> 00:54:59,151
[SPEAKER_01]: He was a fastball hitter.

784
00:54:59,712 --> 00:55:05,036
[SPEAKER_01]: And you could throw him 19 change-ups and he's still hitting for the fastball with the big wagon top.

785
00:55:06,557 --> 00:55:09,019
[SPEAKER_01]: And he's lunging, lunging out.

786
00:55:10,620 --> 00:55:14,864
[SPEAKER_01]: And after he hit this home run, he proved to me that I couldn't hit fastball.

787
00:55:15,724 --> 00:55:19,187
[SPEAKER_01]: I would show him the fastball where he couldn't hit it.

788
00:55:19,888 --> 00:55:22,990
[SPEAKER_01]: But just, and he couldn't wait.

789
00:55:23,707 --> 00:55:34,493
[SPEAKER_01]: He'd hit ground balls into the ground, and if he did, if he'd gotten the ball that sold him inside, if he got good wood on it, he was way out in front.

790
00:55:34,753 --> 00:55:37,274
[SPEAKER_01]: He'd pull it back just a long strike.

791
00:55:37,975 --> 00:55:50,121
[SPEAKER_01]: And I could never understand, even after I left baseball, why other pitchers didn't learn to not throw at the fastball.

792
00:55:50,201 --> 00:55:52,142
[SPEAKER_01]: Don Newcombe, I remember this after.

793
00:55:52,681 --> 00:55:54,823
[SPEAKER_01]: Don Newcombe won 28th of August.

794
00:55:56,104 --> 00:56:01,910
[SPEAKER_01]: Yogi Berra hit three home runs off of Don Newcombe in the World Series that year, and two there in Boston.

795
00:56:02,731 --> 00:56:06,194
[SPEAKER_01]: Newcombe couldn't throw a fastball by him, and he could throw one.

796
00:56:07,195 --> 00:56:11,720
[SPEAKER_01]: But a lot of other pitchers that pitched against Yogi for years, they never did learn how to pitch.

797
00:56:21,717 --> 00:56:25,379
[SPEAKER_02]: Who were some of the toughest hitters in the American league you faced, would you say?

798
00:56:26,039 --> 00:56:29,181
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, I've been asked that question all the time.

799
00:56:30,321 --> 00:56:31,822
[SPEAKER_01]: I'll start in reverse a little bit.

800
00:56:32,622 --> 00:56:44,788
[SPEAKER_01]: The hitters that I loved to pitch to were the big free swingers that swung for the home run, and especially if they couldn't hit the ball up and in.

801
00:56:46,489 --> 00:56:49,210
[SPEAKER_01]: The hitters that gave me trouble, that killed me dead,

802
00:56:50,289 --> 00:56:53,510
[SPEAKER_01]: was the little left-hand here that hit the ball.

803
00:56:53,550 --> 00:56:54,710
[SPEAKER_01]: You couldn't strike him out.

804
00:56:54,750 --> 00:56:58,530
[SPEAKER_01]: He'd hit the ball where it's pitched to the opposite field.

805
00:56:58,550 --> 00:57:06,352
[SPEAKER_01]: Nellie Fox, Johnny Pesky, those kind of guys, they wore me out.

806
00:57:09,032 --> 00:57:14,153
[SPEAKER_01]: But the big freestylers, especially if they couldn't hit the ball up, I loved them.

807
00:57:15,553 --> 00:57:18,634
[SPEAKER_01]: I was sitting in Fenway Park two years ago,

808
00:57:19,363 --> 00:57:21,984
[SPEAKER_01]: after the old-timers came, when they gave us this watch.

809
00:57:22,625 --> 00:57:26,907
[SPEAKER_01]: We're sitting way up here in the box, where they had to run these boxes inside.

810
00:57:26,927 --> 00:57:28,488
[SPEAKER_01]: You'd go out here and watch the voting.

811
00:57:30,749 --> 00:57:32,430
[SPEAKER_01]: Bobby Doerr and I were sitting here.

812
00:57:34,071 --> 00:57:40,634
[SPEAKER_01]: And this Bill Balboni, the big Cleveland, I mean, the first one.

813
00:57:40,654 --> 00:57:41,214
[SPEAKER_02]: Kansas City.

814
00:57:41,595 --> 00:57:42,295
[SPEAKER_01]: Kansas City.

815
00:57:42,595 --> 00:57:45,116
[SPEAKER_01]: Kansas City vote for Balboni.

816
00:57:45,797 --> 00:57:46,737
[SPEAKER_01]: And I stole the door.

817
00:57:47,878 --> 00:57:48,158
[SPEAKER_01]: I said,

818
00:57:49,392 --> 00:57:53,313
[SPEAKER_01]: If I could pitch against nine like this guy, I would still be pitching.

819
00:57:53,933 --> 00:57:59,994
[SPEAKER_01]: But, I told him, if I had to pitch against nine like Wade Boggs, I wouldn't even come to the ballpark.

820
00:58:00,114 --> 00:58:00,914
[SPEAKER_01]: I'd stay at home.

821
00:58:02,014 --> 00:58:04,075
[SPEAKER_01]: And while I'm talking, Balboni struck out.

822
00:58:04,995 --> 00:58:06,755
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the kind of hitter I love.

823
00:58:08,735 --> 00:58:12,636
[SPEAKER_01]: I struck out Rudy York one time in Detroit.

824
00:58:13,276 --> 00:58:14,956
[SPEAKER_01]: He later came to our ball club.

825
00:58:16,257 --> 00:58:18,517
[SPEAKER_01]: Rudy York was a long ball hitter.

826
00:58:20,067 --> 00:58:23,748
[SPEAKER_01]: I struck out Rudy York three times on nine pitches.

827
00:58:23,768 --> 00:58:25,168
[SPEAKER_01]: The first three times was bad.

828
00:58:26,028 --> 00:58:30,109
[SPEAKER_01]: The fourth time he was supposed to hit, they took him out for a pinch hitter.

829
00:58:30,149 --> 00:58:34,690
[SPEAKER_01]: He ripped rapidly from left hand hitting out for a hit for Rudy York.

830
00:58:36,170 --> 00:58:38,270
[SPEAKER_01]: I struck him out three times on nine pitches.

831
00:58:38,350 --> 00:58:41,351
[SPEAKER_01]: I just happened to get it there one of those days.

832
00:58:41,391 --> 00:58:42,771
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't think he even fouled the ball.

833
00:58:44,071 --> 00:58:44,971
[SPEAKER_01]: How about Greenberg?

834
00:58:45,811 --> 00:58:48,912
[SPEAKER_01]: Greenberg was, I didn't pitch against him that much.

835
00:58:49,697 --> 00:58:54,821
[SPEAKER_01]: But when I saw him, he had slowed up somewhat in his reflexes.

836
00:58:55,662 --> 00:59:02,287
[SPEAKER_01]: And you could throw the fastball at him, especially if you crowd him a little bit.

837
00:59:02,487 --> 00:59:04,308
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm not talking about how others...

838
00:59:05,529 --> 00:59:11,334
[SPEAKER_01]: There's pitchers on the Red Sox ball club that ask me, how do you pitch so-and-so?

839
00:59:12,695 --> 00:59:14,036
[SPEAKER_01]: I said, I pitch him inside.

840
00:59:16,198 --> 00:59:19,100
[SPEAKER_01]: And they said, well, I pitch him inside and he wires me out.

841
00:59:19,690 --> 00:59:23,952
[SPEAKER_01]: I said, yeah, but you're pitching him on the inside part of the plate for a strike.

842
00:59:26,193 --> 00:59:30,015
[SPEAKER_01]: And I said, I'm pitching him this far inside the plate of the field.

843
00:59:31,175 --> 00:59:31,936
[SPEAKER_01]: In on his hands.

844
00:59:31,976 --> 00:59:34,977
[SPEAKER_01]: I broke a lot of backs.

845
00:59:35,297 --> 00:59:36,198
[SPEAKER_01]: That was my mate.

846
00:59:36,238 --> 00:59:38,399
[SPEAKER_01]: That's where I wanted to go.

847
00:59:38,739 --> 00:59:43,401
[SPEAKER_01]: Until they proved that it could hit that far.

848
00:59:44,142 --> 00:59:47,143
[SPEAKER_01]: I'll tell you a guy that gave me a lot of trouble for a right hander.

849
00:59:48,471 --> 00:59:54,673
[SPEAKER_01]: that could hit me with his eye closed was George Kell.

850
00:59:55,053 --> 00:59:56,293
[SPEAKER_01]: He was a good hitter.

851
00:59:57,373 --> 00:59:59,894
[SPEAKER_01]: He was a hungry hitter, kind of like Enos Slurr.

852
01:00:00,394 --> 01:00:02,974
[SPEAKER_01]: He never gave up a time to fight away.

853
01:00:03,014 --> 01:00:05,995
[SPEAKER_01]: He was just up here all the time, aggressive all the time.

854
01:00:07,015 --> 01:00:17,738
[SPEAKER_01]: I seen Bobby Doar, he'd come back to bench, angry at himself, said, I just wasn't thinking, I wasn't firing down, I just give up a time to fight away.

855
01:00:18,932 --> 01:00:21,833
[SPEAKER_01]: George Carolina, Florida, they'd never give you a time.

856
01:00:21,873 --> 01:00:24,595
[SPEAKER_01]: Pete Rose, never give us a time to fight away.

857
01:00:25,055 --> 01:00:26,636
[SPEAKER_01]: They're looking for that baseball.

858
01:00:29,237 --> 01:00:35,540
[SPEAKER_02]: Did you keep a book on the other batters, a regular book, or did you just have it all up here?

859
01:00:35,580 --> 01:00:37,901
[SPEAKER_01]: No, I'll tell you what.

860
01:00:38,481 --> 01:00:39,582
[SPEAKER_01]: I had it up here.

861
01:00:43,364 --> 01:00:48,286
[SPEAKER_01]: I had one time back when I was pitching.

862
01:00:49,870 --> 01:00:53,011
[SPEAKER_01]: I would take the line-up for my pitch against the Yankees tomorrow.

863
01:00:54,191 --> 01:00:55,192
[SPEAKER_01]: I'd look at the line-up.

864
01:00:56,772 --> 01:01:06,055
[SPEAKER_01]: After the ballgame, mentally, just like you had it taped here, and right by, I'd be laying down.

865
01:01:06,955 --> 01:01:17,778
[SPEAKER_01]: If I pitched a good ballgame, I could recall each batter, say, went about four times, I could tell you

866
01:01:20,366 --> 01:01:27,188
[SPEAKER_01]: Each time at bat, approximately what the count was, I could tell you where he hit the ball.

867
01:01:28,108 --> 01:01:33,610
[SPEAKER_01]: Each time at bat, I could go through the whole ball clock system, after the ball game.

868
01:01:34,630 --> 01:01:39,751
[SPEAKER_01]: If this man went to bat, I could tell you, well, the first time, DeMargio.

869
01:01:40,752 --> 01:01:42,472
[SPEAKER_01]: Last December, yeah.

870
01:01:43,452 --> 01:01:44,853
[SPEAKER_01]: Ground ball and shortstop.

871
01:01:45,613 --> 01:01:46,813
[SPEAKER_01]: Or double off the wall.

872
01:01:47,193 --> 01:01:49,694
[SPEAKER_01]: I could tell you the time at bat, what he hit.

873
01:01:50,350 --> 01:01:52,891
[SPEAKER_01]: approximately what the count was and where he hit the ball.

874
01:01:55,071 --> 01:01:57,812
[SPEAKER_01]: After the ball game, then try to adjust.

875
01:02:03,974 --> 01:02:11,896
[SPEAKER_00]: I've also read that some pitchers used to study box scores to see how certain hitters did against certain pitchers.

876
01:02:12,016 --> 01:02:13,377
[SPEAKER_01]: Absolutely.

877
01:02:13,437 --> 01:02:15,617
[SPEAKER_01]: I've done this extensively.

878
01:02:15,657 --> 01:02:18,158
[SPEAKER_01]: Watch the box score and see

879
01:02:19,112 --> 01:02:25,817
[SPEAKER_01]: Of course this, it can get carried too far and it can confuse the heck out of you.

880
01:02:26,717 --> 01:02:35,603
[SPEAKER_01]: But I'd like to know if St. Louis Brown is beating somebody, what kind of picture was working against him?

881
01:02:36,524 --> 01:02:38,866
[SPEAKER_01]: And who was successful against him?

882
01:02:39,406 --> 01:02:40,427
[SPEAKER_01]: What type of picture?

883
01:02:41,788 --> 01:02:43,489
[SPEAKER_01]: This says middle section, doesn't it?

884
01:02:44,169 --> 01:02:46,431
[SPEAKER_01]: But also you want to know,

885
01:02:48,437 --> 01:02:54,098
[SPEAKER_01]: if you're pitching, you want to know who's hitting good on that ballclub and who isn't.

886
01:02:57,019 --> 01:03:03,200
[SPEAKER_01]: I would walk out of 275 or 80 innings, I would walk maybe 50.

887
01:03:05,701 --> 01:03:11,902
[SPEAKER_01]: But over half of those would be, to me, not ordered by the manager, would be intentional.

888
01:03:11,922 --> 01:03:12,882
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm pitching around.

889
01:03:16,083 --> 01:03:16,603
[SPEAKER_01]: However,

890
01:03:17,455 --> 01:03:24,000
[SPEAKER_01]: What amazes me, I sit here and watch TV, all these announcers are giving the pitcher credit.

891
01:03:25,862 --> 01:03:34,088
[SPEAKER_01]: And last night they said, well, he worked him outside, then he worked him inside, ball two.

892
01:03:35,429 --> 01:03:39,832
[SPEAKER_01]: The announcer is giving the pitcher credit for doing just exactly what he wanted to.

893
01:03:40,213 --> 01:03:44,676
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, he was trying to throw a strike, trying to get the ball over the plate, that's all he was trying to do.

894
01:03:45,063 --> 01:03:49,365
[SPEAKER_01]: He wasn't working him this way or working him that way.

895
01:03:49,725 --> 01:03:50,125
[SPEAKER_00]: I know.

896
01:03:52,006 --> 01:03:58,949
[SPEAKER_01]: But most of the cases I did, I was throwing for a strike.

897
01:03:58,989 --> 01:04:00,529
[SPEAKER_01]: I wanted to get ahead of that hitter.

898
01:04:01,130 --> 01:04:03,110
[SPEAKER_01]: I didn't nibble, I didn't fool around.

899
01:04:03,931 --> 01:04:07,432
[SPEAKER_01]: And if I ever walked that first man, you might as well put him on a scoreboard.

900
01:04:07,832 --> 01:04:08,533
[SPEAKER_01]: It seemed like it.

901
01:04:09,053 --> 01:04:09,913
[SPEAKER_01]: Score in third.

902
01:04:11,014 --> 01:04:13,915
[SPEAKER_01]: But the times come when, there are the times when

903
01:04:14,570 --> 01:04:16,231
[SPEAKER_01]: I remember this thing.

904
01:04:18,452 --> 01:04:21,593
[SPEAKER_01]: The worst hitting pitcher in American League.

905
01:04:21,773 --> 01:04:25,695
[SPEAKER_01]: I walked him on four pitches leading off three.

906
01:04:25,855 --> 01:04:28,256
[SPEAKER_01]: I was close, but I just couldn't find the play.

907
01:04:28,556 --> 01:04:30,777
[SPEAKER_01]: The last time I just aimed the damn ball.

908
01:04:31,558 --> 01:04:32,838
[SPEAKER_01]: Who was that incidentally?

909
01:04:32,858 --> 01:04:33,578
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't remember.

910
01:04:33,678 --> 01:04:34,919
[SPEAKER_01]: Johnny Brooker?

911
01:04:34,979 --> 01:04:35,519
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't remember.

912
01:04:35,539 --> 01:04:37,160
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, Johnny Brooker.

913
01:04:37,180 --> 01:04:42,422
[SPEAKER_01]: But he was an out man, you might say, a saint.

914
01:04:42,622 --> 01:04:43,763
[SPEAKER_01]: Sometimes I would do this.

915
01:04:45,397 --> 01:04:49,818
[SPEAKER_01]: work extra hard to make that pitcher lead off so I got him.

916
01:04:50,358 --> 01:05:02,341
[SPEAKER_01]: Or if there's two good hitters, left-hand hitters, give me trouble hitting three and four, I'd try my best to split those two so I don't have to face both of them in one inning.

917
01:05:03,241 --> 01:05:04,621
[SPEAKER_02]: What happened to Boo Ferris?

918
01:05:04,861 --> 01:05:07,182
[SPEAKER_02]: He came up, then he went down.

919
01:05:07,502 --> 01:05:09,262
[SPEAKER_02]: Just didn't last long, did he?

920
01:05:09,782 --> 01:05:12,223
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, Boo Ferris was a heck of a boy.

921
01:05:12,943 --> 01:05:13,483
[SPEAKER_01]: He came up,

922
01:05:14,288 --> 01:05:15,309
[SPEAKER_01]: He got out of the service.

923
01:05:16,569 --> 01:05:18,410
[SPEAKER_01]: He was at Randolph Field out there.

924
01:05:19,291 --> 01:05:20,912
[SPEAKER_01]: He came to Boston in 45.

925
01:05:20,972 --> 01:05:24,034
[SPEAKER_01]: He won 20 and so he grew up.

926
01:05:25,975 --> 01:05:28,996
[SPEAKER_01]: From 46 to the end of my career, I roomed with him.

927
01:05:29,016 --> 01:05:34,119
[SPEAKER_01]: He's a fine gentleman, a Southern Baptist or Methodist or something.

928
01:05:35,060 --> 01:05:38,762
[SPEAKER_01]: He never used curse words, never drank smoke or anything.

929
01:05:42,820 --> 01:05:49,044
[SPEAKER_01]: He won 25 and lost 6 in 46.

930
01:05:51,966 --> 01:05:53,687
[SPEAKER_01]: But he came up with arm problems.

931
01:05:56,288 --> 01:06:03,633
[SPEAKER_01]: Now there's a man that could throw the same type of pitch I'm talking about, the Dyson type of throw.

932
01:06:04,013 --> 01:06:04,894
[SPEAKER_01]: His was better.

933
01:06:05,714 --> 01:06:06,615
[SPEAKER_01]: He broke a little bit.

934
01:06:06,895 --> 01:06:09,056
[SPEAKER_01]: But he threw a heavy ball, that old thing.

935
01:06:09,697 --> 01:06:11,338
[SPEAKER_01]: He could throw it and run it.

936
01:06:12,937 --> 01:06:16,740
[SPEAKER_01]: And Boo didn't have a good curveball, didn't have a good changer.

937
01:06:17,440 --> 01:06:21,663
[SPEAKER_01]: But he threw, just warming up with him, the old ball was heavy, kind of boring him.

938
01:06:24,485 --> 01:06:27,227
[SPEAKER_01]: But he came up with some arm trouble problems.

939
01:06:28,348 --> 01:06:32,811
[SPEAKER_01]: He was a good athlete, pretty good hitter.

940
01:06:33,031 --> 01:06:33,791
[SPEAKER_02]: Where does he live?

941
01:06:34,752 --> 01:06:35,573
[SPEAKER_01]: In Mississippi.

942
01:06:35,593 --> 01:06:38,355
[SPEAKER_01]: He's been coaching at some college there.

943
01:06:38,375 --> 01:06:40,616
[SPEAKER_01]: He stayed with the Red Sox for a while as a coach.

